const memCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · The hippocampus: the writer', note: 'New episodic memories (what happened where) are formed here, then consolidated to cortex during sleep. Patient H.M. (hippocampus removed, 1953) could not form new memories — but could learn new skills. Memory is multiple systems.' },
    { math: '2 · Long-term potentiation (LTP)', note: 'Repeated co-activation strengthens a synapse for hours to lifetime ("cells that fire together wire together"). LTP in the hippocampus is the most-studied mechanism in neuroscience — the NMDA receptor is its gatekeeper (calcium influx only when pre- and post-neuron fire together).' },
    { math: '3 · Systems consolidation: the night shift', note: 'During sleep, the hippocampus replays the day\'s episodes to the cortex, which stores them long-term. Sleep deprivation after learning measurably impairs retention — the all-nighter is self-sabotage.' },
    { math: '4 · Reconsolidation: memories rewrite on recall', note: 'Each retrieval returns the memory to a labile state — reconsolidating with current context and emotion. This is why memories drift, why leading questions distort testimony, and why extinction therapy for trauma retrains rather than erases.' },
  ],
};
