<TopicPage slug="subjects/neuroscience/advanced/memory-circuits">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Memory is not stored in one place — it is <strong>distributed across systems, each handling a
    different kind of remembering</strong>: the hippocampus writes episodes, the cortex stores
    them long-term, the cerebellum and basal ganglia hold skills, the amygdala adds emotional
    weight. The circuits lesson builds on the psychology ladder's memory lesson with the
    hardware.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the memory systems and their molecular machinery" widget="equationStep" config={memCfg} />

  <h2>The case studies that mapped memory</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Patient H.M.:</strong> lost the ability to form new episodic memories (1953 surgery) yet improved at mirror-drawing daily without remembering practising — proving procedural and declarative memory are separate systems. Studied for 55 years; his anonymity ended only at his death.</li>
    <li><strong>Kandel's sea slug (Aplysia):</strong> a simple nervous system, one reflex, and the molecular basis of learning visible in the dish — sensitisation tracked to specific synapse changes (Nobel 2000).</li>
    <li><strong>London taxi drivers:</strong> years of navigating "The Knowledge" enlarged their posterior hippocampi (Maguire, 2000) — structural plasticity in adult humans, measured.</li>
    <li><strong>Optogenetics (2005→, Nobel-adjacent):</strong> light-controlled neurons allowed memory traces to be switched on and off in mice — false memories implanted, specific engrams reactivated. Memory research moved from correlation to causation.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Scoville & Milner (1957) on H.M.; Kandel (Nobel 2000); Maguire et al., PNAS (2000); Ramirez & Liu (2013) optogenetic engrams.</p>
</TopicPage>
